WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Gary Goldstein: You know, they are both very
different.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.060
It’s one of the few mediums that you actually
get a possessory credit.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:06.350
You’ve seen Neil Simon’s Plaza Suite.

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:11.120
I mean occasionally you’ll see a Lee Daniel’s
movie and so forth, but by and large the words

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.390
are really so much more the focus and the
writer is really respected so much more in

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:18.390
theater.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.199
It’s a different kind of medium altogether.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:26.429
In theater you get to write stories that are
very personal and very intimate and very small

00:00:26.429 --> 00:00:32.610
and really character driven where the words
are just important and it’s all about dialogue

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.610
and all of that.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:40.010
I had a funny experience when…well I taught
screenwriting for many years for a group called

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.240
Writer’s Bootcamp.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.920
I had a student who was a great woman.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:51.130
She was an actress but she was always kind
of bucking me about structure and like “Why

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.470
do I have to structure things this way?”
and “Can’t you loosen up in terms of this

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.470
and that?”

00:00:56.470 --> 00:01:00.230
and I just tried to explain to her that structure
and storytelling worked a certain way.&nbsp;

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:04.680
So she came to see the play, my very first
play (it was a long time ago) and she walked

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.020
out and said, hugging me “I loved the play
but I loved the words.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:15.359
I love that you who is teaching me structure,
you wrote these amazing words that were just

00:01:15.359 --> 00:01:16.359
words.

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:17.359
It was all words and character.”

00:01:17.359 --> 00:01:20.819
And as an actress that was her priority.

00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:28.310
And it was really very eye-opening to me that
really how different words are when it comes

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:35.159
to theater because movies are about showing
not telling very often because you have the

00:01:35.159 --> 00:01:39.359
ability to show a lot of things without having
to explain a lot of things.

00:01:39.359 --> 00:01:44.350
Whereas in theater, it’s much more limited
in terms of what you can show so you end up

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.539
telling more and that can be very interesting.

00:01:47.539 --> 00:01:50.229
Writers enjoy telling stories (in which you
can do on stage).

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:52.780
So I like that.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:02:01.189
The live part of it…I write a lot of comedies
and to be in a live audience where they are

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:07.189
laughing and responding or crying or whatever
and to be around them live…even different

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.319
from being in the theater watching people
watch your movie, it’s different.

00:02:11.319 --> 00:02:17.590
There is something about the live experience
that’s very moving and compelling.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.959
So that part is great.

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:26.689
I think as a writer I always encourage screenwriters
to write plays as well.

00:02:26.689 --> 00:02:32.079
I wrote my first play at a time where I had
a little time and I needed to get something

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:34.090
done that was mine.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.140
I didn’t have to worry about selling.

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.230
I didn’t have to worry about it fitting
any particular paradigms.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.670
I could tell the story that I wanted to tell.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.280
And it just happened to have worked out.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.860
I was very proud of the play.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:51.480
We actually had a stage reading of it and
I was surprised at the response to it and

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.000
then I was encouraged to get the play produced.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.550
And we did and it was a fantastic experience.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:03.329
You also (as the writer) get to deal very
directly with the actors and the director

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:07.840
in a very different way that you do in TV
and film.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.370
It’s really more of that team effort at
that particular moment.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.159
Particularly the new play, that you are still
discovering the words, working on the words

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:19.790
as you’re in rehearsals.

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:25.549
Whether it’s a film I’ve written or a
play, I’m very interested in hearing what

00:03:25.549 --> 00:03:27.180
the actors have to say.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.560
Just because you write a piece of dialogue
a certain way doesn’t mean that somebody

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:39.099
who has to say it, can say it that way or
a comfortable way.

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:43.500
In my head, I hear it a certain way and what
comes out of somebody’s mouth, may be different.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.040
It’s always fascinating to see which lines
really don’t play or are too tongue-twistery

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.879
or really don’t make as much sense as they
should.

00:03:51.879 --> 00:03:56.761
You have that option when you’re in rehearsals
live at that moment and it’s kind of an

00:03:56.761 --> 00:03:57.761
evolutionary process.

00:03:57.761 --> 00:04:01.230
Film Courage: We understand you have a stage
play that you’re producing coming up in

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:02.230
Beverly Hills?

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:03.230
Gary Goldstein: Yes.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.230
I have a play.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.230
I’m not producing it.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:06.670
It’s a theater call Theatre 40 in Beverly
Hills.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:09.969
They’re producing it and have been there
a long time.

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:12.719
It’s a really good 99-seat theater.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.760
And they have a subscription and they do a
season.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.870
This is the fifth out of the sixth plays of
their 2016-2017 season.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.630
It’s called April, May and June.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:30.250
It will run March 16th through the middle
of April (16th).

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:37.430
It’s the story of three adult sisters named
April, May and June in their forties (they’re

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.530
a year apart).

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:41.840
And they convene at their late mother’s
house, which is the house they grew up in

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.770
to go through the very last of her things.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:51.919
And in doing so they discover something about
the mother that blows their mind.

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:58.539
It’s a surprise but in a small way connected
to them and their own past.

00:04:58.539 --> 00:05:03.000
And it’s kind of how they come up coming
to terms of the image of their mother and

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.759
the whole idea of we never quite know people
as much as we think, particularly parents.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:14.449
And the sisters who are very different and
very connected close women, their relationship

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:18.840
is really improved by this afternoon they
spend in their childhood house.

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:23.370
It’s just how they come to terms with each
other and the image of the mother and they

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.039
can finally close up the house and move on.

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:30.680
Film Courage: When you sat down to write that
[stage play], were you in the same mindset

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.650
of ‘I just want to do something that is
my own’ or was there some other impetus?

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:40.670
Gary Goldstein: In this particular case, and
this happens every so often with a film script

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:42.360
particularly, I’ll come up with a title.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.250
And the title April, May and June just came
into my head for some reason.

00:05:46.250 --> 00:05:49.020
And I thought “That’s a good title, I
should do something with that.”

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.020
And I don’t know?

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.580
I just started writing it and it ended up
becoming a stage play.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.220
I wasn’t exactly sure where it was going
when I started.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.699
At a certain point I realized it was going
to be this revelation about the mother.

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:05.480
I realized the revelation I wanted it to be
and it kind of evolved from there.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:09.040
So it was kind of a unique process that way.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:14.199
As opposed to having it completely structured
and a full outline and knowing where it was

00:06:14.199 --> 00:06:15.199
going.

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:19.910
I mean I wrote it and edited it and ultimately
got it to the place where I felt comfortable

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:22.289
sending it out or showing it.

00:06:22.289 --> 00:06:26.330
But it was a very different process from the
screenwriting that I do.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.220
Film Courage: Is that common where you will
just come up with a title and work backwards?

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.599
Gary Goldstein: Once and awhile I do that.

00:06:31.599 --> 00:06:32.599
Once and awhile.

00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:34.450
You know it happens.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:40.050
This play is comedy drama and I hope some
good laughs in it, but there are also some

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:41.509
tears as well.

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:46.639
In a lot of the straight comedies that I do,
you know just higher concept comedies, I’ve

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:50.590
come up with titles and then figured out the
story to do with it.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.409
If there was no story attached, I wouldn’t
continue writing it.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:06:56.729
There are movies that I’ve written where
I’ve ended up changing the title many times

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:01.530
over things, so it just depends.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.319
People say “How do you get the idea to write
this?” or “Where did you come up with

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:06.319
this?

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:07.400
Who thought of something like that?”

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:09.169
Well you know, we are writers and we write.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:14.139
We’re storytellers or creators but sometimes
they come from strange places.

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:16.390
It can be from something you’ve observed.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:18.060
It can be from people that you know.

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:22.009
It can be from just coming up with a phrase
or in this case a title.

00:07:22.009 --> 00:07:23.009
So it’s just one of those weird things.

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:24.009
Question for the viewers: Have you written
a stage play?

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:24.012
What inspired it?

